# Draft:Warsaw College of Engineering and Health

}}
The Warsaw College of Engineering and Health is a private higher education institution with a practical profile, specializing in medical, exact, and natural sciences, as well as health sciences.
The College was established pursuant to the Act of June 26, 1997, on Higher Vocational Schools. The College’s founding act was drawn up on September 24, 2003. The Warsaw College of Engineering and Health is entered in the register of private universities under number 284:.
The College’s founder and Chancellor is PhD Marcin Jarosław Geryk, DSc, professor at the Jagiellonian University.
History
The idea to establish the College arose in 2000. The College was established on November 4, 2003, pursuant to the Act of June 27, 1997, on Higher Vocational Schools.
After obtaining permission and being entered into the register, the College began offering undergraduate studies in spatial planning and management. In 2007, it obtained permission to offer engineering studies in environmental protection, and in 2008, by decision of the minister responsible for higher education, it was authorized to offer undergraduate studies in computer science and administration.
In October 2015, the College changed its name to the Warsaw College of Engineering and Health. Since October 1, 2015, its main building is located at ul. Bitwy Warszawskiej 1920 nr 18 in Warsaw (Campus 1).
In subsequent years, the College expanded its educational offer, focusing on practical education, combining engineering with health sciences. The curriculum includes programs such as cosmetology and dietetics.
The College also organizes popular science and educational events, including the Health Knowledge Academy and World Health Day celebrations.
On June 18, 2025, the College signed a letter of intent on cooperation with the 6th Masovian Territorial Defense Brigade. It is also developing cooperation with economic and institutional partners, including through socio-economic councils, business cooperation consortia, and the initiative of the College’s patronage of schools.
College Authorities
The College authorities include
* Rector – Sebastian Grzyb, PhD;
* Chancellor – Marcin Jarosław Geryk, PhD, DSc, professor at the Jagiellonian University, also the College's founder;
* Vice-Rector for Quality of Education and Teaching – Mateusz Gemba, PhD, DSc;
* Vice-Rector for Relations with the Socio-Economic Environment – Agata Wolska, MD, PhD;
* Rector’s Plenipotentiary for Science – Angelika Muchowicz, MD, PhD, DSc.
Fields of Study
The College is a non-faculty institution. It offers programs in the fields of medical and health sciences:
* Cosmetology, first-cycle studies, full-time and part-time;
* Cosmetology, second-cycle studies (training paths: Clinical Cosmetology; Bioesthetic Cosmetology), full-time and part-time;
* Dietetics, first-cycle studies (training paths: Sports Dietetics; Applied Dietetics; Clinical Dietetics), full-time and part-time;
* Dietetics, second-cycle studies (training paths: Food and Nutrition Safety; Dietoprophylaxis and Dietotherapy; Psychodietetics), full-time and part-time;
* Nursing, first-cycle studies;
* Public Health, first-cycle studies, full-time and part-time.
The College also offers programs in the fields of exact and natural sciences:
* General Chemistry, first-cycle studies, full-time and part-time;
* Cosmetic Chemistry, first-cycle studies, full-time and part-time;
* Chemistry, second-cycle studies, full-time and part-time;
* Healthcare Engineering and Promotion (training paths: Cosmetic Product Safety; Food Safety), first-cycle studies, full-time and part-time;
* Cosmetic Technology, second-cycle studies, full-time and part-time.
The College also offers postgraduate studies through the Collegium of Postgraduate Studies, in Polish and English, in the following fields:
* Training Programming and Nutrition Strategies in Sports;
* Chemistry and Formulation of Cosmetics;
* Health Coaching;
* Cosmetic Design;
* Diabetology and Gastroenterology for Dietitians;
* Diagnostics in Cosmetology;
* Dietetics;
* Dietetics and Supplementation in Sports;
* Clinical Dietetics;
* Health Education;
* Food Design;
* Gastronomy and Dietetics;
* Hygiene and Epidemiology;
* Food and Nutrition Hygiene;
* Quality and Safety of Cosmetic Products;
* Bioesthetic Cosmetology;
* Holistic Cosmetology;
* Oncological Cosmetology;
* Organization and Management in Healthcare;
* Perfume Making;
* Podology;
* Psychodietetics;
* Food technology and microbiology;
* Cosmetics production technology;
* Cosmetological Trichology;
* Well-Being and Health at Work;
* Food Management and Safety;
* Green Chemistry.
Specialized education:
* Training Programming and Nutrition Strategies in Sports.
The prominent presence of study programmes in these two areas in the College’s educational activities means that medical and health sciences programmes have a strong exact sciences component.
Education Quality
The mission of the Warsaw College of Engineering and Health is to educate students and support their professional development through the implementation of study programmes that address the needs of economic, social, and cultural development.
In 2007, the College introduced an Internal Education Quality Assurance System. Its operation is overseen by the College authorities and the Office for Education Quality Improvement. The system includes, among other things, regular surveys of students, faculty, staff, and representatives of the socio-economic environment, as well as initiatives aimed at improving the quality of education.
The socio-economic councils also participate in the education quality assurance process, collaborating with the College on organizing training courses and specialized workshops, reviewing study programmes, and supporting the organization of internships.
The College also implements projects in collaboration with national and European institutions, including those financed by the European Union and the Ministry of Science and Higher Education. An example is the project Modification of the second-cycle study program in the field of Dietetics at the Warsaw College of Engineering and Health, co-financed by the European Funds for Social Development 2021–2027 (FERS), under Priority I “Skills”, Measure 01.05 “Skills in higher education”.
International Cooperation
The Warsaw College of Engineering and Health conducts activities to internationalize its education. The College collaborates with foreign higher education institutions within the framework of the Erasmus+ program.
Bilateral agreements have been signed with higher education institutions in many European countries, e.g., Bulgaria, Cyprus, Croatia, Czechia, France, Greece, Spain, Lithuania, Latvia, North Macedonia, Portugal, Romania, Slovenia, Turkey, and Italy.
As part of the Erasmus+ program, students, academic staff, and administrative staff participate in educational and training mobilities at partner higher education institutions abroad. At the same time, the Warsaw College of Engineering and Health accepts students, research and teaching staff, and administrative staff from partner higher education institutions. The College also participates in Blended Intensive Programmes (BIPs) under the Erasmus+ program, which combine physical mobility with an online learning component.
Academic and Teaching Facilities
The Warsaw College of Engineering and Health conducts teaching activities on two campuses in Warsaw.
* Campus 1 is located at Bitwy Warszawskiej 1920 r. nr 18 (Ochota district). It houses the library, lecture halls, laboratories, and administrative offices.
* Campus 2 is located at Aleje Jerozolimskie 195A (Włochy district). It houses teaching laboratories, a fitness center, the office of the Head of the Institute of Health for Nursing, and the Medical Simulation Center, which opened in 2026.
